#
# One reader of the kernel log, not two.
#
# /proc/kmsg hands a message to whoever reads it first and to nobody else.
# OpenWrt's logd keeps it open for logread; rsyslog's imklog module opens it
# too, and the production image ships both. They then race for every kernel
# message, and logd is single-threaded: uloop tells it the fd is readable,
# imklog takes the message first, and logd's read blocks with nothing left to
# read. It never returns to its loop again.
#
# What that costs is out of all proportion to the cause. A blocked logd stops
# draining /dev/log, so every daemon still holding a connection to it hangs on
# its next log line - and the ones that do are the ones started before rsyslog
# took the socket over: dropbear, netifd, wpad. Measured on hardware
# 2026-08-17, on the first image ever built with both the production package
# set and the mesh layer:
#
#     logd            wchan=syslog_print         ubus log read: timeout
#     netifd          wchan=unix_wait_for_peer   LuCI: "XHR request timed out"
#     hostapd         wchan=unix_wait_for_peer   no AP on the air at all
#     wpa_supplicant  wchan=unix_wait_for_peer
#     dropbear child  wchan=unix_wait_for_peer   no SSH, banner never sent
#
# The node answered ping and served static pages, so from outside it looked
# alive. Dropping imklog and waking logd with one kernel message brought every
# one of them back within seconds, with nothing restarted.
#
# It is a race, which is the worst part: rsyslog and logd have coexisted in
# production images all along and mostly logd won. A fault that appears on one
# boot in some and never on the next is the kind that reaches a customer.
#
# rsyslog keeps everything else - /dev/log through imuxsock, the remote input,
# the files under /var/log. Only the second pair of eyes on the kernel log
# goes, and logread works again because of it.

[ -f /etc/config/rsyslog ] || exit 0
uci -q get rsyslog.syslog.modules | grep -q imklog || exit 0

uci del_list rsyslog.syslog.modules='imklog'
uci commit rsyslog

# Only if it is already running - at first boot the init system has not
# started it yet and this would race with that.
[ -n "$(pidof rsyslogd)" ] && /etc/init.d/rsyslog restart >/dev/null 2>&1

exit 0
